ll it honesty"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for an active challenge?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Plan and do a safe 15-minute active challenge for yourself or your family with at least three water breaks","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing the best athletes ignore thirst","Name a water break and explain why it matters only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies an active challenge to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about an active challenge with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating an active challenge on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using an active challenge on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for an active challenge. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with an active challenge, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing the best athletes ignore thirst.. Correction: Rewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w14-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise an active challenge.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use an active challenge with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a water break and explain why it matters.","I can practise a thirst rating independently on a new prompt.","I can notice resting safely needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes.","options":["An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about an active challenge with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute water break. Zoe rates her thirst from 1 to 5: 2 before, 4 after round 3, then 1 after drinking. She learns to drink before her thirst reaches a 4.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA challenge has the rule ''no water breaks until you have finished''. Rewrite the rule to make it safe and say why.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps the challenge safe and inclusive.","explain":"An active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you feel very thirsty, drink slowly, stop if you feel dizzy or unwell and rest in the shade on hot days. A challenge can be against yourself, such as keeping your own drink diary for a week, rather than against other people.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Activity and water breaks.","caption":"Drink every round.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"