e spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute water break. Zoe rates her thirst from 1 to 5: 2 before, 4 after round 3, then 1 after drinking. She learns to drink before her thirst reaches a 4. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Thirst ratings.","caption":"Drink before 4.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Thirst</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2 before, 4 after</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">back to 1</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about an active challenge with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Here is a more helpful example: Example challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute water break. Zoe rates her thirst from 1 to 5: 2 before, 4 after round 3, then 1 after drinking. She learns to drink before her thirst reaches a 4.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''Take a water break after every round, and anyone can stop to rest or drink at any time.'' Water breaks protect the body, and the choice to stop keeps the challenge safe and inclusive.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing the best athletes ignore thirst. Drinking regularly and resting are part of safe, healthy performance. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about an active challenge with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAn active water challenge links movement with hydration: do short bursts of activity, take water breaks and see how the body changes. Plan breaks before you feel very thirsty, drink slowly, stop if you feel dizzy or unwell and rest in the shade on hot days. A challenge can be against yourself, such as keeping your own drink diary for a week, rather than against other people. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 2: Active water challenge","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Healthy food and nutrition","week":14,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample challenge: four rounds of 2 minutes of activity (star jumps, jogging on the spot, hopping, marching), each followed by a 1 minute water break. Zoe rates her thirst from 1 to 5: 2 before, 4 after round 3, then 1 after drinking. She learns to drink before her thirst reaches a 4.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Activity and water breaks.","caption":"Drink every round.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Round</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2 minutes of activity</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Break</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1 minute of water</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Thirst ratings.","caption":"Drink before 4.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill